ii
Usage: ii -s SERVER [-p PORT] [-n NICK] [-f FULLNAME] [-i DIR]

Minimal IRC client. It has no interface: it maps the connection onto the
filesystem. Each channel becomes a directory holding an `in` FIFO you
write to and an `out` file you read.

	-s SERVER	host to connect to
	-p PORT		port (default 6667)
	-n NICK		nickname
	-f FULLNAME	real name field
	-i DIR		root of the tree (default ~/irc)

	ii -s irc.example.org -n xos -i ~/irc &
	echo '/j #chan' > ~/irc/irc.example.org/in
	tail -f ~/irc/irc.example.org/#chan/out

Because it is just files, every text tool on this system works on it --
grep the log, tail the channel, echo to send.

See also: tail, grep, mkfifo
